This page is a table of contents, page 3 of the index to the continuation of 'La Longue Marche' (ms. pp. 650 bis–787). It covers two sections.

§52, 'Approximations de Lie des systèmes de Galois-Teichmüller'. The original title, crossed out, was 'Enveloppes schématiques des L-groupes et TL-groupes'. Its subsections are:
1°) the notion of L-group and TL-group, discrete case (p. 737);
2°) the profinite case (p. 744, or 749);
3°) Lie approximations of Galois-Teichmüller systems (p. 748). This replaces the crossed-out 'Discrétisation et homomorphismes admissibles'.
4°) Lie approximations induced by a 'schematic' Lie approximation (p. 758).

In modern terms, Grothendieck has just treated schematic envelopes of discrete groups in §50. These are group-scheme hulls over Spec Z built from unipotent and quasi-unipotent sections, close to Malcev, pro-unipotent or relative completions. He has also worked through the Fermat tower example in §51. In §52 he wants to replace the Galois-Teichmüller system by linear, Lie-theoretic data. That system is the tower of profinite Teichmüller groups π̂_1(M_{g,n}) with the outer action of Gal(Q̄/Q). The Lie data would be Lie algebras or group schemes attached to discrete groups ('L-groups') and to Teichmüller-type groups ('TL-groups'). He handles both discrete and profinite versions, together with compatible 'admissible' homomorphisms. He also expects a schematic Lie approximation to induce the Lie approximations of the system.

This is the programme later realised in part as:
- Ihara's Lie algebra of stable derivations and pro-ℓ / graded Lie approaches;
- Drinfeld's grt and GRT (1990);
- Deligne's motivic fundamental group of P^1 minus three points (1989);
- Hain–Matsumoto relative completions of mapping class groups and weighted completions of Galois actions.

§53, 'Le groupe de Teichmüller spécial ST_{1,1} ≃ GL(2,Z)~, et le groupe SN_{1,1}' (p. 750, probably 760). Its subsections are:
1°) the differentiable action of the special Teichmüller group (provisional statement);
2°) the groups GL(2,R)^{±}~ and SL(2,R)~, i.e. universal-cover-type central extensions of the real linear groups;
3°) GL(2,Z)~, the inverse image of GL(2,Z) in that extension;
4°) the isomorphism GL(2,Z)~ ≃ ST_{1,1};
5°) a digression on coverings of groups of spaces, and on relations between topological and differentiable groups of surfaces;
6°) left empty.

In modern language, ST_{1,1} is presumably the mapping class group of a genus-1 surface with one marked point carrying extra 'special' rigidification, such as a tangent direction or boundary. The SL part of such a group is the braid group B_3 ≅ SL(2,Z)~, the preimage of SL(2,Z) in the universal cover of SL(2,R). Here it is extended to GL including orientation reversal. The digression concerns comparing Homeo and Diff of surfaces and their covering groups; the homotopy equivalences are classical (Hamstrom, Earle–Eells).
